== First-Author Papers
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_atone_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_atone_2026.png" alt="ATONE: symptom-driven unlearning in a federated vehicular network" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Initiating Removal from Operational Symptoms in Federated Vehicular Networks
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang, Qiang Duan</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">Preprint, 2026</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Connected vehicles train a driving model by federated learning, so a faulty contribution usually<br />
      surfaces only as a delayed symptom on the road, naming neither the vehicle nor the round. ATONE<br />
      makes unlearning a term of the aggregation rule: it projects driving errors and reported symptoms<br />
      onto a subspace of recent global increments, scores every contribution in one backward pass, and<br />
      suppresses suspected effects each round before a one-round probe confirms which to eliminate.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_herd_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_herd_2026.png" alt="HERD: knowledge lineage and delta-closure under mutual distillation" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Tracing Knowledge Lineage for Federated Unlearning under Mutual Distillation
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19072" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang, Guodong Wang</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">arXiv:2610.19072, 2026 (Preprint)</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      An edge server and nearby devices train language models of different sizes by mutual distillation,<br />
      so correcting one model is not enough &mdash; the students that copied the removed knowledge teach it<br />
      back on their next upload. HERD reads a knowledge lineage graph off the transfers the server already<br />
      performs, counts how many times each residual will be copied, and corrects the cheapest set of<br />
      holders that bounds what survives.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_sweep_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_sweep_2026.png" alt="SWEEP: structured deletion units on a channel knowledge map" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Structuring and Merging Deletion Demands on Crowdsourced Channel Knowledge Maps
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">Preprint, 2026</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      A channel knowledge map built from uplink pilots has to forget the measurements a fault corrupted,<br />
      but dropping the whole terminal throws away its good ones and the raw reports are long gone. SWEEP<br />
      names what to forget as a structured deletion unit over terminals, rounds, grids and bands, clears<br />
      the matching ledger blocks, re-sounds the affected grids, and merges pending demands so that one<br />
      campaign serves several.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_shade_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_shade_2026.png" alt="SHADE: classical-shadow unlearning for quantum federated learning" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Federated Unlearning of Quantum Data via Classical Shadows
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19077" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang, Cong-cong Xing</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">arXiv:2610.19077, 2026 (Preprint)</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Quantum federated learning cannot replay the data it is asked to forget, because a quantum state is<br />
      consumed when it is measured and cannot be cloned. SHADE has each node record a classical shadow of<br />
      every training state as training happens, estimates the gradient and curvature of the forget data<br />
      from those shadows at any later parameters, and removes their effect with a federated Newton<br />
      correction. Only classical estimates cross the network.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_mute_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_mute_2026.png" alt="MUTE: influence echo in a self-improving federated agent network" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      When Unlearning Fails: Reliable Data Deletion under Post-Training in Agent Networks
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28829" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang, Liang Dong</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">arXiv:2607.28829, 2026 (Preprint)</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Self-improving agent networks keep training after deployment, so deleted data can echo back<br />
      through the trajectories collected in later rounds. MUTE estimates that downstream influence<br />
      from a lightweight server ledger, clears the current residue with a forget&ndash;retain update,<br />
      and contains high-influence retained trajectories so the echo does not return.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_ease_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_ease_2026.png" alt="EASE framework overview" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      EASE: Federated Multimodal Unlearning via Entanglement-Aware Anchor Closure
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.00733" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Beining Wu, Jun Huang</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">arXiv:2605.00733, 2026 (Preprint)</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Forgotten image&ndash;text alignments survive federated unlearning through three residual anchors.<br />
      EASE closes each one: bilateral displacement of the visual and language branches,<br />
      Cosine&ndash;Sine decomposition that isolates forget-exclusive update directions,<br />
      and a direction-selective Forget Lock that bounds residual drift across rounds.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig stretch" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/arxiv_tilp_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/arxiv_tilp_2026.png" alt="TiLP hardware testbed" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Application-Aware Twin-in-the-Loop Planning for Federated Split Learning over Wireless Edge Networks
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26105" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Beining Wu, Jun Huang, Shiwen Mao</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">arXiv:2604.26105, 2026 (Preprint)</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Federated split learning at the wireless edge couples bandwidth, power, split-layer placement,<br />
      compression and terminal participation under per-round deadline and memory limits. TiLP scores<br />
      candidate decisions in a cross-domain digital twin &mdash; network, training and task sub-twins,<br />
      each calibrated at its own time scale &mdash; before committing them to the real system.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/ieee-network_fedslu_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/ieee-network_fedslu_2026.png" alt="FedSLU testbed results" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      Toward Trustworthy Federated Unlearning for Mobile Autonomous Systems
      [<a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/11581296" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Jun Huang</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">IEEE Network, 2026</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Wireless federated learning on mobile autonomous systems has to delete data without taking the<br />
      service down. FedSLU locates the layers that carry the target knowledge by tracking weight<br />
      cluster drift, retrains sparsely on retained data, and overwrites those layers with low-rank<br />
      updates, while dual pipelines keep learning and unlearning running concurrently.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~
~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="pubcard">
  <a class="pubcard-fig" href="https://github.com/GUYhen/ZihaoDing.github.io/blob/master/docs/paper_image/full/icdcs_scale_2026.png" target="_blank" title="Click for the full-resolution figure">
    <img src="paper_image/icdcs_scale_2026.png" alt="SCALE framework overview" />
  </a>
  <div class="pubcard-body">
    <div class="pubcard-title">
      SCALE: Sensitivity-Aware Federated Unlearning with Information Freshness Optimization for Mobile Edge Computing
      [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22589" target="_blank">PDF</a>]
    </div>
    <div class="pubcard-authors"><b>Zihao Ding</b>, Beining Wu, Jun Huang</div>
    <div class="pubcard-venue">IEEE ICDCS, 2026</div>
    <div class="pubcard-desc">
      Unlearning at the mobile edge is usually coarse and ignores how stale the remaining knowledge is.<br />
      SCALE works at two levels: historical contribution analysis pinpoints the layers a departing<br />
      client shaped most, then adaptive sparsification edits weight subgroups inside them,<br />
      trading information freshness against forgetting strength.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
~~~

